INT. LUNAR TRANSIT LOUNGE - LONG PERIOD

A curved observation hall hewn from basalt. Banks of pale seating bolted to the floor. A wall-sized window frames Earth, a blue coin hung in black. Overhead, signage in six languages announces delays that have no end.

The air smells of recycled coffee and warm plastic. A cleaning drone hums past, brushing at invisible dust.

RHIANNON "RHI" ACHTERBERG-KOMA, 30s, in a faded flight jacket, sits at a corner table nursing a cold tube of coffee. Her boots are up on a spare chair. She has been here a long time.

A man in a grey pressure suit approaches with a tray. THADDEUS "TAD" LOWENSTEIN-OSEI, 50s, graying, polite. He carries two tubes. He stops a respectful distance away.

TAD
Is this seat taken?

RHI
The chair is. The seat isn't.

She moves her boots. He sits. Sets the second tube in front of her.

RHI (CONT'D)
I didn't order that.

TAD
I thought you might have a second thirst.

RHI
I have a second everything. It's what happens when you wait.

TAD
Which flight?

RHI
Mars Direct. Twice rescheduled.

TAD
Mine's the Ceres shuttle.

RHI
Sixteen days late.

TAD
You've been checking.

RHI
Everyone checks. It's the hobby.

They sip. Outside, a transport crosses the lunar horizon, trailing a thin plume. Earth turns in the window, slowly, blue and white.

TAD
I used to come up here for work. Now I come for the view.

RHI
It's always the same view.

TAD
That's the comfort.

RHI
The comfort is that I know what I'm missing.

TAD
And what's that?

RHI
Weather.

He laughs. The drone returns, brushes around their table, and sweeps away.

TAD
What's your trade?

RHI
Soil. I'm an agronomist. I teach dust to grow things.

TAD
Does it listen?

RHI
Not to me. To the right microbe, maybe.

TAD
I fix valves.

RHI
Somebody has to.

TAD
Somebody does. Mostly me, on bad days.

She turns the tube in her fingers. Peers at the label.

RHI
Why Ceres?

TAD
My daughter's there. Three years. She keeps asking me to visit and I keep telling her it's a long trip.

RHI
Is it?

TAD
It was, until I decided to go.

A soft chime. Overhead, a board flickers. A line changes. Both of them look up.

RHI
Yours?

TAD
Mine.

RHI
Boarding?

TAD
In forty minutes.

Rhi nods. Lifts her tube in a small salute. He raises his in answer.

RHI
To soil, then.

TAD
To valves.

They drink. Earth turns, unhurried. He rises, takes his tray, and pauses at the edge of the table.

TAD (CONT'D)
If you ever see Ceres. The garden domes are poor. They'd listen to you.

RHI
Is that an invitation?

TAD
It's a valve repair request. Disguised.

She smiles for the first time. He nods and walks away across the basalt floor.

Rhi puts her boots back up on the chair and settles to wait.
